   "text": "A home Wi-Fi network is like a highway for data, allowing your laptop to receive and send information to the internet. When you watch a video on your laptop, the process begins with the router, which acts as a traffic controller for your home network. The router connects to the internet via a cable or fiber optic line, and it has a built-in access point that broadcasts Wi-Fi signals.\n\nThe access point sends out radio waves, which are invisible to the naked eye, but they carry the data packets that make up the video stream. These packets are small chunks of data that travel through the air to your laptop. Each packet contains a piece of the video, and they are sent in a specific order so that your laptop can reassemble them into a complete video stream.\n\nWhen your laptop receives these packets, it decodes them and assembles the video. This process happens quickly, so you don't notice any delay. However, if two devices in your home try to transmit data at the same time, it can cause a bit of a traffic jam. This is because the radio channels used by Wi-Fi are shared among all devices in the network. When two devices transmit at the same time, their signals can interfere with each other, leading to dropped packets or slower data transfer"
